{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1364694-modern-foundations-and-pillars","id":1364694,"title":"Modern Foundations and Pillars","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-02-01T22:23:46-08:00","id":1364694,"title":"Modern Foundations and Pillars","content":"I am a really big fan of the Foundations and Pillars system from DA:M. I love the system to pieces and I have seen references to people having some write ups for either more Foundation/Pillars sets and some references to write ups for modern traditions Foundations and Pillars.<br /> I was hoping if anyone had any of those they might be willing to share them with me.<br /> I really, really, /really/ don't want this to turn into an argument or debate over which is better Spheres or Pillars. Please, just don't. I personally love Pillars, but Spheres are also great.<br /> Again, any write ups, modern, historical, any are good for me.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25226-knightfenrirwulfhart","name":"KnightFenrirWulfhart","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"41"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-01T23:00:39-08:00","id":1364698,"title":"","content":"I don't actually have write-ups; but my <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/441968-pillars-for-the-modern-crafts\">Pillars for the Modern Crafts</a> thread has a number of ideas to consider. <br /> <br /> Another approach to consider would be to tie each set of Foundation and Pillars to a single Practice: that is, rather than associating them with <i>factions</i>, associate them with <i>Practices</i>. Modus and Forma isn't an Order of Hermes thing; it's a High Ritual thing. Divinity and Archangels wouldn't be a Messianic Voices/Celestial Chorus thing; it would be a Faith thing. Spontaneity and the Seasons wouldn't be an Old Faith thing; it would be a Witchcraft thing. And so on. <br /> <br /> That said, it needn't be a strictly one-to-one relationship: if two Practices are very similar to each other, then they might share the same Foundation and Pillars; and there's nothing wrong with a single Practice having more than one associated Foundation and Pillars setup.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-02T20:27:07-08:00","id":1364918,"title":"","content":"I love Pillar and Foundation too, but I think they are harder to implement in more modern mage because the route to Ascension logically progressed to a more standardized approach to magic. It's value seems to be in a intermediate step between Linear and Dynamic magic (because Foundations and Pillar contain elements of both which is one thing I like about it) or it can reflect a different way to get around some of the (over-rated IMHO) complaints about mage-hybrids (which is what V20's Black Hand book went with and works really well.)<br /> <br /> I know there's at least one floating around that contained a mostly-complete conception for Dark Age Akashics (I know its been mentioned here multiple times) Given the ties to linear magic you could take inspiration from Sorcerer, Hekau from Mummy... heck most of the other game systems have ways to implement mages within their 'game systems and some are more flexible than others (Hunter had an interesting approach in the Spellbound enemy book, esp for hedge magic, and VtM gave them a form of thaumaturgy) I also liked some of revised's ideas for minor spheres and other alternate systems which could help develop foundation/pillars. If you know the spellcasting 'concept' and you can develop four basic 'areas' you could tie them to one main 'sphere' then add a couple minor spheres to flesh them out. I haven't looked at Dark Age Mage and their foundation/pillar creation rules but no reason you still coudln't use those either.<br /> <br /> Edit: I also think Foundation and Pillar would mesh well with an 'Orphan' concept (or some of the Disparates): something 'non-traditional' that might evolve from 'dabbling' with a bunch of different mystical concepts (something that might evolve into spheres later... but it may not. Orphans seem like they'd be good for tying into a lot of the alternate magic systems presented in the Storyteller's handbook","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25533,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25533-mister_dunpeal","user":"Mister_Dunpeal","date":". 02-02-2020, 11:48 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25533-mister_dunpeal","name":"Mister_Dunpeal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"308"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-02T22:41:59-08:00","id":1364949,"title":"","content":"Let me push back on the notion that Foundation and Pillars exist in a middle ground between Hedge Magic and the Arete and Spheres system: they don't. Foundation and Pillars are essentially the same kind of system as Arete and Spheres. <br /> <br /> 1. Both Foundation/Pillars and Arete/Spheres operate on a design paradigm where you have a power stat which describes in a general way how potent your magic is, and a set of \u201cflavor stats\u201d that define in broad strokes what <i>kinds </i> of things your magic can do. <br /> <br /> 2. In both cases, raising your power stat lets you discard the tools you use to perform magic. Arete does so now slowly that Foundation (which is no surprise when you consider that Arete goes to 10 while Foundation only goes to 5); but in both cases, once you've maxed out your power stat, you don't need to use any of your magical tools anymore. <br /> <br /> 3. Both Spheres and Pillars are rated up to five dots, and each dot provides some broad strokes as to what sorts of things become possible that weren't before. In particular, neither system requires you to learn individual spells, rotes, rituals, or whatever else you want to call them. <br /> <br /> There are a couple of ways that Foundation and Pillars are more restrictive than Arete and Spheres; but these have nothing in common with what makes Hedge Magic more restrictive than Arete and Spheres:<br /> <br /> 1. There are only four Pillars per Foundation (except when there aren't, as in the case of the Forma of the Order of Hermes and the Craftmasons). The Hedge Magic/Sorcery system has <i>more</i> Paths than the Sphere system has Spheres, by a significant margin; <i>it</i> is more restrictive than Spheres in this regard in that the Paths are more narrowly focused than the Spheres are. By contrast, Pillars tend to be as broad or even broader than Spheres. As well, Hedge Magic requires you to learn individual spells (or whatever you want to call them); as stated above, Pillars don't.<br /> <br /> 2. Foundation and Pillars are effectively hardwired to a Practice, with the choice of tools to be used being specified with each Pillar, and the nature of the Foundation tending to reflect a philosophy commonly associated with that Practice. The Sorcery system\u2026 isn't; not really. It uses a setup similar to earlier editions of Mage where your style of magic is determined largely by the faction you've joined, and at most this reflects on your Paths in the sense that different factions favor different Paths. <br /> <br /> Foundation and Pillars is firmly in the same camp as Arete and Spheres; just implemented differently. <br /> <br /> ---<br /> <br /> Again, I'd recommend pairing Foundations and their Pillars with Practices: Divinity gets associated with Faith; Spontaneity gets associated with Witchcraft; Modus gets associated with High Ritual Magic; Sensitivity gets associated with Shamanism; and so on.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 02-11-2020, 02:45 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-03T00:26:52-08:00","id":1364955,"title":"","content":"I feel Foundations are more the philosophy of the Group with Pillars being the Practices. With Traditions being Broader than Crafts/Fellowships incorporating Multiple Practices under One Foundation.. with the Traditions as a whole facilitating this to a Greater Degree. Or at least if we are attempting to use Pillars/Foundation to simulate something resembling the Modern Traditions","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-03T00:37:00-08:00","id":1364959,"title":"","content":"Fair enough: Foundations probably have more to do with what M20 calls Paradigms and what older editions called Theory or Philosophy, while Pillars have more to do with the Practices. Older editions of the game wed Philosophy/Theory and Practice directly to the factions (as in \u201cpick a faction, and that will tell you what your Philosophy/Theory and Practice is like\u201d), whereas M20 treats Paradigm and Practice as standalone units, with the factions being described in terms of what Paradigms and Practices they subscribe to (i.e., \u201cpick a Paradigm and Practice, and that will tell you what sorts of factions you might be compatible with\u201d).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 02-03-2020, 03:39 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-03T01:54:33-08:00","id":1364970,"title":"","content":"While I really appreciate the advice for making new ones or tying them to Practices, I think I might have not explained my initial post sufficiently.<br /> I am genuinely just looking for write ups because I like reading them. ^_^;<br /> I don't play much these days but I like lore and character development so I just collect stuff for my personal enjoyment.<br /> While the Spheres and Arete serve the Mage The Ascension game fine, to me it always felt more shoehorned in, in actual lore. Whereas Foundations and Pillars fit what the lore said more accurately, at least to me. That's the problem with retroactive prequels, though, you have had time to think of more things to explain other things which might not make complete sense with preexisting lore.<br /> I, /personally/, see the Sphere system as a purely game mechanic thing, separation of gameplay and lore. I get where people come from when they say that the Sphere system is the Traditions' way of working together, but it comes across as, well, like the Technocracy. Very specific manner in which Will is focused, defined into distinct and separated studies that can be shown and followed by many regardless of, and in spite of, personal aptitude and belief. (Ironically, I love the Technocracy for the most part.)<br /> But! I do like Sphere/Arete because it is a simple way of stating out NPCs. The few times I've played as Storyteller I let my friends tell me what their characters magic could do and we worked together to build some basic Foundations and Pillars for them (and kept them from the other players beyond what was already unlocked so they would feel surprised by what each character could do) while I kept all NPCs running Spheres/Arete for simplicity. Made them all feel special and unique and powerful. Plus it really made them work together to come up with plans and roleplay.<br /> As I said before, both are very good systems for different things, I just prefer, and feel more immersed with Foundations and Pillars than Spheres and Arete. But thats just a personal preference. <br /> Really, these days I don't play much but I really enjoy just reading write ups. ^_^<br /> I really do appreciate the advice though, I'm not good with crunch as me and my friends are more storybased than mechanics based, but I might give it a try and make some writeups for some characters I have floating around in my brain! <br /> So, thanks!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25226,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25226-knightfenrirwulfhart","user":"KnightFenrirWulfhart","date":"02-03-2020, 04:55 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25226-knightfenrirwulfhart","name":"KnightFenrirWulfhart","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"41"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-06T14:56:38-08:00","id":1365850,"title":"","content":"V20's guide to the black hand had pillar based magic, called archaic sorcery included as well as a darker modern version of the Chakravanti (euthanatos) called the Idran, it also basically revised the pillar system for the 20th-anniversary rules.<br /> <br /> Majorly it linked each pillar to four keywords which defined what exactly the pillar could affect (fire, demons, ghosts, connections, growth), making the whole thing more comprehensive. It added clearly defined spell factors to define how, for example, damage, trait buffs/penalties and area of effect worked, as well as situational modifiers for casting in combat. Range was made simply either line of sight or sympathetic. Foci were linked to Foundation and each dot over 1 removed the need for foci for one pillar and getting four dots in foundation and a pillar let you get a specialty in one focus and keyword respectively. It did trim out any inherent bonuses for having a high foundation though. It also included rules for how blood bonds could be worked with magic. Finally, it added revised scourge rules.<br /> <br /> Keywords worked roughly like this<br /> \u2022 <span style=\"font-family:Calibri, sans-serif\">Awareness of the keywords of a pillar, Protection against passive keyword effects (ex: heat but not fire)</span><br /> \u2022\u2022 <span style=\"font-family:Calibri, sans-serif\">add bonuses or penalties related to keywords, call (but not compel or create) keywords, inflict bashing damage, interact with keywords directly</span><br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022 <span style=\"font-family:Calibri, sans-serif\">manipulate a keyword, inflict lethal damage, summon, ban or trap a keyword, damage/destroy keywords, embody or traverse the keyword, make another interact with a keyword directly</span><br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 <span style=\"font-family:Calibri, sans-serif\">transform a keyword completely, command or control a keyword, transcend mortal limits, make another embody or traverse a keyword</span><br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 <span style=\"font-family:Calibri, sans-serif\">unleash widespread keyword, transcend divinely imposed limits, make massive incontestable transformations</span><br /> <br /> While I haven't converted the old DA: Mage pillars to the revised system it wouldn't be terribly hard to do.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3729,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3729-darkfoxdev","user":"Darkfoxdev","date":". 02-06-2020, 06:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3729-darkfoxdev","name":"Darkfoxdev","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"161"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-07T03:05:06-08:00","id":1365977,"title":"","content":"Ooooo! I didn't know about that. I'll admit, I'm not very into Vampire so thats why I never knew but other than removing the special bonuses for Foundations that sounds a/lot/ nicer for my brain to work with! <br /> Thank you for letting me know! I'll definitely check that out! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25226-knightfenrirwulfhart","name":"KnightFenrirWulfhart","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"41"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-07T07:10:53-08:00","id":1366008,"title":"","content":"I don't recall seeing the summary of the Pillars that <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3729-darkfoxdev\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3729\" data-vbnamecard=\"3729\">Darkfoxdev</a> provides here in the book in question. But no matter; <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3729-darkfoxdev\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3729\" data-vbnamecard=\"3729\">Darkfoxdev</a> provides it here. Also, the Scourge rules are remarkably simple: take the successes you get on the Backlash roll and craft a suitable spell to inflict on the caster, using the caster's Pillars as a rough guideline for what sorts of mischief the Backlash can cause.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-07T15:17:41-08:00","id":1366128,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1364694-modern-foundations-and-pillars?p=1366008#post1366008\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't recall seeing the summary of the Pillars that <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3729-darkfoxdev\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3729\" data-vbnamecard=\"3729\">Darkfoxdev</a> provides here in the book in question. But no matter; <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3729-darkfoxdev\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3729\" data-vbnamecard=\"3729\">Darkfoxdev</a> provides it here. Also, the Scourge rules are remarkably simple: take the successes you get on the Backlash roll and craft a suitable spell to inflict on the caster, using the caster's Pillars as a rough guideline for what sorts of mischief the Backlash can cause.</div> </div> </div> </div>The summary of what each pillar does per dot was my own work based on what each of the Idran's pillars did and trying to assess the design being used, it's been awhile so I forget exactly <i>why </i>I worked so hard to break these down, I think I may have been trying to stat out Lilith for some unfathomable reason and decided she should know Archaic Sorcery.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3729,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3729-darkfoxdev","user":"Darkfoxdev","date":". 02-07-2020, 06:36 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3729-darkfoxdev","name":"Darkfoxdev","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"161"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-07T15:41:56-08:00","id":1366139,"title":"","content":"Well, it's nicely done. If I ever get around to the M20 Old Magics STV pdf that I'd like to do, I'd like to explicitly include this up front. (Of course, that's a long way down the road, if it ever happens at all.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-10T17:42:09-08:00","id":1366890,"title":"","content":"If there was one thing I would change for the V20 Foundation/pillar adaptation is to have fount so it was more in line with Avatar ratings (or the Mana rating for some sorcerers from WoD: Sorcerer) It just felt odd that an archaic mage would somehow accumulate or use more quintessence than a modern mage.. in the same timeframe. Even if the methods are more archiac and less flexible than spheres I don't think it would explain that discrepancy. (or you could go the other way and allow Avatars to behave more like Founts from Dark Age: Mage)<br /> <br /> Edit: Also Dataweaver I think I kinda see what you're trying to get at by saying that the Foundation/Pillar system isn't an intermediary step... but I'm not sure I'd agree with that interpretation. I mean yeah there's some correlation but you would expect that if its a medium step. and Dark Age Mage is pretty clear that Sphere and Foundation magic aren't the same:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dark Ages Mage</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The interconnectedness of ideas and concepts that crops up in the wake of the first gatherings of the Nine Mystic Traditions will eventually lead to a much more encompassing philosophy one that sees the similarities in what traditional wizards, woodland druids and ancestral shamans do, rather than the differences. But for now, those are gulfs that can be traversed by only a handful of exceptional people. Among other things, the result of this is that essentially no consensus exists beyond certain fundamental precepts (things fall down, the sun rises in the east and sets in the west, etc.) Although this means that the broad spectrum of ideas necessary to beget thoughts of Ascension is virtually unheard of, it also means that each mage is ultimately bound only by the limits of her own ambition and ability to achieve. To put it another way: <b>The magic of the Dark Ages is dynamic, within a certain set of static frameworks that are so (and will remain thus for a while yet) by convention, not nature.</b></div> </div> </div> </div>So its quasi-dynamic, but with far more limits (intentionally) and less flexibility than the spheres. And this is by design (thats why learning a whole different foundation/pillar system would be more challenging than learning a different tradtiion through sphere magic, as I see it.) And I see the Foundation/Pillar system likewise being more akin to the 'minor spheres' optional setup from the storyteller's handbook (which is also kinda dynamic and has parallels to the sphere system but.. isn't too similar to sphere magic. In fact they flat out say its meant to be a midpoint between sphere and linear magic. But I'd also say minor spheres are MORE flexible - or differently flexible - than foundation and pillar.) <br /> <br /> I'd also point out that there's a big gray area when it comes to sphere dots in whether or not you still think archspheres are a thing or not. If they are, then sphere magic has far more potential in other ways than foundation/pillars.. which again seems to be by design. <br /> <br /> I'll acknowledge there's tons of wiggle room to quibble over these details but that is honestly how I see it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25533,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25533-mister_dunpeal","user":"Mister_Dunpeal","date":". 02-10-2020, 08:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25533-mister_dunpeal","name":"Mister_Dunpeal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"308"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-11T11:38:41-08:00","id":1367047,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1364694-modern-foundations-and-pillars?p=1364949#post1364949\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Let me push back on the notion that Foundation and Pillars exist in a middle ground between Hedge Magic and the Arete and Spheres system: they don't. Foundation and Pillars are essentially the same kind of system as Arete and Spheres.<br /> <br /> 1. Both Foundation/Pillars and Arete/Spheres operate on a design paradigm where you have a power stat which describes in a general way how potent your magic is, and a set of \u201cflavor stats\u201d that define in broad strokes what <i>kinds </i> of things your magic can do.<br /> <br /> 2. In both cases, raising your power stat lets you discard the tools you use to perform magic. Arete does so now slowly that Foundation (which is no surprise when you consider that Arete goes to 10 while Foundation only goes to 5); but in both cases, once you've maxed out your power stat, you don't need to use any of your magical tools anymore.<br /> <br /> 3. Both Spheres and Pillars are rated up to five dots, and each door provides some broad strokes as to what sorts of things become possible that weren't before. In particular, neither system requires you to learn individual spells, rotes, rituals, or whatever else you want to call them.<br /> <br /> There are a couple of ways that Foundation and Pillars are more restrictive than Arete and Spheres; but these have nothing in common with what makes Hedge Magic more restrictive than Arete and Spheres:<br /> <br /> 1. There are only four Pillars per Foundation (except when there aren't, as in the case of the Forma of the Order of Hermes and the Craftmasons). The Hedge Magic/Sorcery system has <i>more</i> Paths than the Sphere system has Spheres, by a significant margin; <i>it</i> is more restrictive than Spheres in this regard is that the Paths are more narrowly focused than the Spheres are. By contrast, Pillars tend to be as broad or even broader than Spheres. As well, Hedge Magic requires you to learn individual spells (or whatever you want to call them); as stated above, Pillars don't.<br /> <br /> 2. Foundation and Pillars are effectively hardwired to a Practice, with the choice of tools to be used being specified with each Pillar, and the nature of the Foundation tending to reflect a philosophy commonly associated with that Practice. The Sorcery system\u2026 isn't; not really. It uses a setup similar to earlier editions of Mage where your style of magic is determined largely by the faction you've joined, and at most this reflects on your Paths in the sense that different factions favor different Paths.<br /> <br /> Foundation and Pillars is firmly in the same camp as Arete and Spheres; just implemented differently.<br /> <br /> ---<br /> <br /> Again, I'd recommend pairing Foundations and their Pillars with Practices: Divinity gets associated with Faith; Spontaneity gets associated with Witchcraft; Modus gets associated with High Ritual Magic; Sensitivity gets associated with Shamanism; and so on.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> A more recent book(admittedly a vampire one) explicitly says that foundation CAN go over 5. Maybe the writers did not include it because in that game foundation has actual effects once you reach the highest levels. To be honest, like most first edition books dark ages mage was a bit shaky in terms of mechanic.<br /> <br /> Anyway, if you want your fireball to do the same amount of damage that an Arete 10 mage's will, you definitely do need foundation 10 even though you also add the pillar to the dice pool(remember that the actual math are done differently)<br /> <br /> The amount of pillars depends on the philosophy of the group in question. It is no secret that some are more versatile than others. Some subgroups can have entirely different ones. For instance germanic old faith are probably much closer to the valdermen philosophically. At least from what we know of real life germanic paganism i think. Likewise remember that the only thing needed for you to join the order of hermes is to swear their (entirely political) oath.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> [QUOTE=Mister_Dunpeal;n1366890]If there was one thing I would change for the V20 Foundation/pillar adaptation is to have fount so it was more in line with Avatar ratings (or the Mana rating for some sorcerers from WoD: Sorcerer) It just felt odd that an archaic mage would somehow accumulate or use more quintessence than a modern mage.. in the same timeframe. Even if the methods are more archiac and less flexible than spheres I don't think it would explain that discrepancy. (or you could go the other way and allow Avatars to behave more like Founts from Dark Age: Mage)<br /> <br /> Edit: Also Dataweaver I think I kinda see what you're trying to get at by saying that the Foundation/Pillar system isn't an intermediary step... but I'm not sure I'd agree with that interpretation. I mean yeah there's some correlation but you would expect that if its a medium step. and Dark Age Mage is pretty clear that Sphere and Foundation magic aren't the same:<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> So its quasi-dynamic, but with far more limits (intentionally) and less flexibility than the spheres. And this is by design (thats why learning a whole different foundation/pillar system would be more challenging than learning a different tradtiion through sphere magic, as I see it.) And I see the Foundation/Pillar system likewise being more akin to the 'minor spheres' optional setup from the storyteller's handbook (which is also kinda dynamic and has parallels to the sphere system but.. isn't too similar to sphere magic. In fact they flat out say its meant to be a midpoint between sphere and linear magic. But I'd also say minor spheres are MORE flexible - or differently flexible - than foundation and pillar.)<br /> <br /> I'd also point out that there's a big gray area when it comes to sphere dots in whether or not you still think archspheres are a thing or not. If they are, then sphere magic has far more potential in other ways than foundation/pillars.. which again seems to be by design.<br /> <br /> I'll acknowledge there's tons of wiggle room to quibble over these details but that is honestly how I see it. [/QUOTE<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> The reason founts are more effective in spending quintessence is that they are more closely attuned to their mages due to the psychopomps doing their job. As for mages regenerating quintessence with fount it's because it was more abundant&quot; in the air&quot; back then<br /> None of which should apply to modern mages.<br /> <br /> All of these limitations that you mention DO exist in Ascension. It's just sidewaves and not enforced by the rules. Hermetics are not supposed to be good with middle umbrood.. but there are no mechanics for it. Taftani are &quot;bad&quot; with life magic, but there are no mechanics either<br /> <br /> Even though their real life groups(hermeticism is a real thing after all) are not supposed to be hyper efficient in all the magic that they (claim to) do and what i mentioned were simply two examples.<br /> <br /> Again a more recent book implies archpillars do exist.. but that's another discussion<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1492,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","user":"mark","date":". 02-11-2020, 02:45 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-12T03:51:17-08:00","id":1367190,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1364694-modern-foundations-and-pillars?p=1367047#post1367047\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Anyway, if you want your fireball to do the same amount of damage that an Arete 10 mage's will, you definitely do need foundation 10 even though you also add the pillar to the dice pool(remember that the actual math are done differently)</div> </div> </div> </div>With the exception of Forces, M20 Arete does successes x 2 levels of damage when casting a damaging spell. Foundation 5/Pillar 5 can do as much damage as that because the damage increases with the level of the Pillar, and while they start doing successes x1 of damage, they can do successes x 2 damage at latter levels (though perhaps you wouldn't call that a mere &quot;fireball&quot;).<br /> This changes with specific Rotes and different editions, but I don't think one can definitely say that Arete 10 damage = hypothetical Foundation 10 damage","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 02-12-2020, 06:54 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-19T06:28:15-08:00","id":1368907,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1364694-modern-foundations-and-pillars?p=1367047#post1367047\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A more recent book(admittedly a vampire one) explicitly says that foundation CAN go over 5. Maybe the writers did not include it because in that game foundation has actual effects once you reach the highest levels. To be honest, like most first edition books dark ages mage was a bit shaky in terms of mechanic.</div> </div> </div> </div>I assume you're referring to the V20 Tal'mahe're book that dealt with Archaic sorcery? Would you have a quote or at least a page number you can provide for that, as I can't remember that specific claim being made in it? <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The reason founts are more effective in spending quintessence is that they are more closely attuned to their mages due to the psychopomps doing their job. As for mages regenerating quintessence with fount it's because it was more abundant&quot; in the air&quot; back then<br /> None of which should apply to modern mages. </div> </div> </div>Perhaps I wasn't clear but I was speaking in the context of a modern mage. Given psychopomps were still linking Avatars with Mages (rather than the Avatars doing it themselves because the psychopomps were gone) as well as the different conditions as far as consensual reality went (magic was only starting to weaken - ie Tremere - and magical sources were still more plentiful, both of which was different than in modern times.) Archaic sorcery from the Tal'mahe're V20 though still uses the fount rules as outlined in Dark Age: Mage, which seems a bit odd given how both modern sorcrers AND mages work. You'd think archaic sorcerers in a modern age wouldn't be able to store/channel more energy than a modern mage. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> All of these limitations that you mention DO exist in Ascension. It's just sidewaves and not enforced by the rules. Hermetics are not supposed to be good with middle umbrood.. but there are no mechanics for it. Taftani are &quot;bad&quot; with life magic, but there are no mechanics either<br /> <br /> Even though their real life groups(hermeticism is a real thing after all) are not supposed to be hyper efficient in all the magic that they (claim to) do and what i mentioned were simply two examples. </div> </div> </div>Perhaps, but the limits are more explicitly defined (And enforced) in the foundation/spheres setup vs the more fluid and open ended approach of sphere magic. Which again seems intentional and I don't think there's a problem with that. Sphere magic should be 'better' than Foundation magic, at least in some ways. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Again a more recent book implies archpillars do exist.. but that's another discussion </div> </div> </div>Whilst there is doubtless room to argue over the relevance of archspheres in 'modern' Mage, it was worth mentioning simply because it reflects another way that sphere magic could end up more powerful than foundation magic without optional rules or similar. Although Archmasters bring their own drawbacks if we're 'going there' too.<br /> <br /> \u200b<br /> <br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1364694-modern-foundations-and-pillars?p=1367190#post1367190\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">With the exception of Forces, M20 Arete does successes x 2 levels of damage when casting a damaging spell. Foundation 5/Pillar 5 can do as much damage as that because the damage increases with the level of the Pillar, and while they start doing successes x1 of damage, they can do successes x 2 damage at latter levels (though perhaps you wouldn't call that a mere &quot;fireball&quot;).<br /> This changes with specific Rotes and different editions, but I don't think one can definitely say that Arete 10 damage = hypothetical Foundation 10 damage</div> </div> </div> </div>Thats a good point. The Arete roll vs the Foundation + Pillar role (nevermind the small differences between the two systems) create alot of differences (you can have pillars higher than your foundation and still cast powerful magic, you just have to pay quintessence to do so. I'm not sure if MtA had a comparable rule since it was all based on Arete outside optional rules.) Conversely I don't think Foundation magic (even hermetics) had that +1 success bonus for forces which can be a small (but still relevant) difference in power for a sphere mage over an archaic sorcerer/foundation mage, at least when it comes to 'single' sphere vs single foundation spells.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25533-mister_dunpeal","name":"Mister_Dunpeal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"308"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-19T09:25:15-08:00","id":1368949,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mister_Dunpeal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1364694-modern-foundations-and-pillars?p=1368907#post1368907\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I assume you're referring to the V20 Tal'mahe're book that dealt with Archaic sorcery? Would you have a quote or at least a page number you can provide for that, as I can't remember that specific claim being made in it?</div> </div> </div> </div>No, surprisingly it\u2019s in a section of Tome of Secrets, about mage crossover mechanics in DAV, you can find its page in content<br /> <br /> But it\u2019s in a vampire book and a rather simpler crossover rules than original ones","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24560,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24560-rock113","user":"Rock113","date":". 02-19-2020, 12:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24560-rock113","name":"Rock113","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24560_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-20T23:39:32-08:00","id":1369286,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Rock113</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1364694-modern-foundations-and-pillars?p=1368949#post1368949\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No, surprisingly it\u2019s in a section of Tome of Secrets, about mage crossover mechanics in DAV, you can find its page in content<br /> <br /> But it\u2019s in a vampire book and a rather simpler crossover rules than original ones</div> </div> </div> </div>Additionally,<br /> <br /> P74, Tome of Secrets<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> An Awakened witch possesses a Foundation rating of 1\u20135 dots (though a few legendary figures may possess ratings of 6 or higher) and one to four Pillars, also rated at 1\u20135 dots (again, legends may possess higher ratings, similar to vampire elders). </div> </div> </div>But again, I suspect if the author has full understanding of DAM, Pillar system is very different from Spheres'.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24560-rock113","name":"Rock113","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24560_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-21T15:12:27-08:00","id":1369455,"title":"","content":"I was thinking about it lately and I realized that Arete is literally just a newer Foundation and the Spheres are just vaguer Pillars. The whole idea of there being only 4 Pillars isn't real in world, its just there for mechanical balance. I don't believe there's really any true metaphysical difference between them. Mechanically it comes from the fact that Spheres and Arete were developed first. <br /> I mean Foundations and Pillars are actually must better to conceptualize Ascension with, and it fits lore much better to me.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25226-knightfenrirwulfhart","name":"KnightFenrirWulfhart","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"41"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-23T11:20:10-08:00","id":1369878,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1364694-modern-foundations-and-pillars?p=1367190#post1367190\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> With the exception of Forces, M20 Arete does successes x 2 levels of damage when casting a damaging spell. Foundation 5/Pillar 5 can do as much damage as that because the damage increases with the level of the Pillar, and while they start doing successes x1 of damage, they can do successes x 2 damage at latter levels (though perhaps you wouldn't call that a mere &quot;fireball&quot;).<br /> This changes with specific Rotes and different editions, but I don't think one can definitely say that Arete 10 damage = hypothetical Foundation 10 damage</div> </div> </div> </div>To be more precise, M20 allows you to do successes x 3 levels of damage of you get more than 5 successes i think. Which i find OP to be honest<br /> <br /> Let's say you have vires 5 foundation(mudus i think) 5. You lore 10 dice at difficulty...9. oups. On average this is one success = 2 damage as per vires 5. <br /> <br /> Now an arete 10 mage rolls at a difficulty of 7. That is an average of 3 successes = 6 levels of damage. Even if using revised rules it's 5 levels of damage(+1 for using forces,. but even if we ignore that it is still 4 levels of damage)<br /> <br /> That's why the pillars need foundation to reach 10<br /> <br /> Of course this makes non damaging spells(such as transformation) a lot more powerful, but is balanced by the fact that combining more than one pillar imposes a much higher difficulty, whereas the ascension mage does not have this benefit/drawback<br /> <br /> it's not just my opinion, that's the actual math","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-26T07:22:21-08:00","id":1370548,"title":"","content":"Mmm, I see you have a point, but those figures you're using are somewhat vague...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Let's say you have vires 5 foundation(mudus i think) 5. You lore 10 dice at difficulty...9. oups. On average this is one success = 2 damage as per vires 5.<br /> <br /> Now an arete 10 mage rolls at a difficulty of 7. That is an average of 3 successes = 6 levels of damage. Even if using revised rules it's 5 levels of damage(+1 for using forces,. but even if we ignore that it is still 4 levels of damage) </div> </div> </div>You're focusing on the fireball example, and you're quite right there...but the thing it's that to rebuke my argument you're then comparing that example using a Sphere level at 3 with the damage of a Pillar at level 5.<br /> <br /> IYou're comparing not only Arete and Foundation, but different <i>levels </i>here, <i>whereas </i><i>I mentioned one specific level</i>. And, the thing is, <i>you can do damage with Sphere level at 5</i> <i>and you can compare that damage with Pillar level at 5</i>.<br /> <br /> If you use Sphere level at the same level of the Pillar, 5, to cast your &quot;damaging spell&quot; (say, you're using Prime 5 to damage) ... the difficulty would be arround 9, with 5 dice averaging 1 success (50% less that when using a Foundation/Pillar at the same level) and with Arete 10 you would be causing the same damage as the Foundation 5/Pillar 5 mage.<br /> <br /> And that's maths too...<br /> <br /> There are occasions where Pillar magick at Foundation 5 will do more damage than Sphere magick at Arete 5, and cases where Arete will do more damage, and a lot of cases where both will do the same damage. Muddled more when you factor different editions (1e deserves a honorary mention, as it's the one that deviates the most) and Rotes that have altered damage<br /> <br /> That being said, &quot;the actual math&quot; shows a correlation there, as you say. Just not as strong as to talk about equality<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Of course this makes non damaging spells(such as transformation) a lot more powerful, but is balanced by the fact that combining more than one pillar imposes a much higher difficulty, whereas the ascension mage does not have this benefit/drawback </div> </div> </div>How it's that &quot;balanced&quot;? There's no way with &quot;the actual math&quot; to unequivocally afirm that such disparate things as &quot;penalties&quot; trough multy-pillar casting and the potential utility of &quot;non damaging&quot; magick balances itself...<br /> <br /> More so, you can cast &quot;non damaging&quot; magick with one Pillar or various and you can cast &quot;non damaging&quot; magick with one Sphere or several (and you can do the same with damaging spells, too). With magick being so broad in it's aplication, I think there's hardly a direct correlation that you can &quot;model&quot; with maths here. <hr /><br /> All in all, I don't think the thing you're saying is *wrong*, per se...I admit there's a mathematical correlation of sorts, but it's far from math proving equality and balance. Maybe I'm being too nitpicky with this = thing, IDK. I just don't think Foundation <i>needs</i> to mirror Arete nor reach the same levels to keep a &quot;balance&quot; (that never existed) in Mage.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> To be more precise, M20 allows you to do successes x 3 levels of damage of you get more than 5 successes i think. </div> </div> </div>That's how Cruzade did damage, not M20 (at least not as per the table of p. 504).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 02-26-2020, 10:40 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-04T10:44:08-08:00","id":1372750,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1364694-modern-foundations-and-pillars?p=1370548#post1370548\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Mmm, I see you have a point, but those figures you're using are somewhat vague...<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> You're focusing on the fireball example, and you're quite right there...but the thing it's that to rebuke my argument you're then comparing that example using a Sphere level at 3 with the damage of a Pillar at level 5.<br /> <br /> IYou're comparing not only Arete and Foundation, but different <i>levels </i>here, <i>whereas </i><i>I mentioned one specific level</i>. And, the thing is, <i>you can do damage with Sphere level at 5</i> <i>and you can compare that damage with Pillar level at 5</i>.<br /> <br /> If you use Sphere level at the same level of the Pillar, 5, to cast your &quot;damaging spell&quot; (say, you're using Prime 5 to damage) ... the difficulty would be arround 9, with 5 dice averaging 1 success (50% less that when using a Foundation/Pillar at the same level) and with Arete 10 you would be causing the same damage as the Foundation 5/Pillar 5 mage.<br /> <br /> And that's maths too...<br /> <br /> There are occasions where Pillar magick at Foundation 5 will do more damage than Sphere magick at Arete 5, and cases where Arete will do more damage, and a lot of cases where both will do the same damage. Muddled more when you factor different editions (1e deserves a honorary mention, as it's the one that deviates the most) and Rotes that have altered damage<br /> <br /> That being said, &quot;the actual math&quot; shows a correlation there, as you say. Just not as strong as to talk about equality<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> How it's that &quot;balanced&quot;? There's no way with &quot;the actual math&quot; to unequivocally afirm that such disparate things as &quot;penalties&quot; trough multy-pillar casting and the potential utility of &quot;non damaging&quot; magick balances itself...<br /> <br /> More so, you can cast &quot;non damaging&quot; magick with one Pillar or various and you can cast &quot;non damaging&quot; magick with one Sphere or several (and you can do the same with damaging spells, too). With magick being so broad in it's aplication, I think there's hardly a direct correlation that you can &quot;model&quot; with maths here. <hr /><br /> All in all, I don't think the thing you're saying is *wrong*, per se...I admit there's a mathematical correlation of sorts, but it's far from math proving equality and balance. Maybe I'm being too nitpicky with this = thing, IDK. I just don't think Foundation <i>needs</i> to mirror Arete nor reach the same levels to keep a &quot;balance&quot; (that never existed) in Mage.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> That's how Cruzade did damage, not M20 (at least not as per the table of p. 504).</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> By &quot;balanced&quot; i did not mean it in the mathematical sense, bur rather a more abstract &quot;give and take&quot; . <br /> I.e your anima 5 man-to-pig spell is backed by 10 dice instead of arete 5 (or 15 instead of 10 if you go by the foundation 6+ method) and this is why as an abstract &quot;drawback&quot; complex spells have a higher difficulty<br /> Or at least this is my explanation for the complex spell increased difficulty. No idea if this is what the writers had in mind<br /> <br /> But if we assume that arete+ foundation goes only up to 10 total:<br /> <br /> 1) You do make an important point by mentioning prime 5.<br /> Perhaps, in that case the rest of the pillars should have similar effects. I.e with anima 5 you can cause 2 levels instead of one. In fact, some pillars do just that. Maybe it has to do with the fact that pillar descriptions are very &quot;skimpy(like i said, it's a first edition system)<br /> Likewise i imagine that using vires 5 to kill a single person instead of everyone is doable<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> I am not sure how good this system is,though it might be a pretty good one indeed.. I guess it allows beginner mages to more easily pull out straightfoward non-asskicking related magickal effects, whereas with arete 3 they might be struggling<br /> What worries my a little is that it makes &quot;unofficial&quot; damage a lot easiter. E.g teleporting someone a hundred feet above, sealing a guy's respiratory system etc<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> 2)Given that ascension's mages will eventually(a tiny few of them anyway) reach arete 10 and therefore have the best of both worlds i would provide a method for Pillar-using mages to eventually reduce the complex spells' difficulty. Maybe by mastering all if their pillars plus a foreign one? *<br /> Of course there is still the issue of them having a higher difficulty ( 9 other than 7) compared to their arete 10 counterparts, but i guess they have the &quot;abstract&quot; advantage if high foundation benefits(such as the Hermetics being able to use 2 willpower points for successes) <br /> Though the old Faith would need a new Foundation benefit in that case<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Hehe, it's worth mentioning that with arch-pillars they gain a one-die advantage again , but if you are playing THAT kind of game i'd just throw the golden rule at it and be done rather than attempt to work around that one too<br /> <br /> <br /> A little of-subject, but am i the only one who interprets rotes as NEVER counting as harried spells? Cause they don't use the usual -1 difficulty benefit here and a lot of the fluff seems to imply this as being the case<br /> I am also inclined to think like that because the dark ages system us quite similar to Ars Magica and this is how it works in that game<br /> <br /> Just check M20. You are right(although, it;s not just sorcerer's crusade, but also second edition)<br /> <br /> <br /> Still, i do prefer revised's method of damage. E.g 4 damage for three successes instead of 6. Mages are potent as it is.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Oh, i'm sure i've asked this before, but how do i separate the text iam quoting into many parts? Like you did in the text i am quoting right now","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-05T04:25:13-08:00","id":1372933,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 2)Given that ascension's mages will eventually(a tiny few of them anyway) reach arete 10 and therefore have the best of both worlds i would provide a method for Pillar-using mages to eventually reduce the complex spells' difficulty. Maybe by mastering all if their pillars plus a foreign one? *<br /> Of course there is still the issue of them having a higher difficulty ( 9 other than 7) compared to their arete 10 counterparts, but i guess they have the &quot;abstract&quot; advantage if high foundation benefits(such as the Hermetics being able to use 2 willpower points for successes)<br /> Though the old Faith would need a new Foundation benefit in that case<br /> <br /> Hehe, it's worth mentioning that with arch-pillars they gain a one-die advantage again , but if you are playing THAT kind of game i'd just throw the golden rule at it and be done rather than attempt to work around that one too </div> </div> </div>I'm accustomed to Mage being a little unbalanced (well, more like a lot :P ) in all it's iterations. It comes with the territory when the limit it's &quot;imagination&quot; + loose parameters. That being said, and as I admited before, I see the correlation you mention does exist - and most likely was intended.<br /> <br /> It's just not something I would place a lot of weigth on.<br /> <br /> I have an idea to model the world of Archmastery as that of those few (crazy &amp; powerful) mages that have learned(or created?) other Foundations (and thus can combine the advantages of them, not to mention the advantages of Pillars cattering different concepts) - if they end causing a somewhat lower amount of dmg in spite of said advantages...well, as long as both can make EPIC feats of magick, and as long as the damage it's &quot;enough&quot; to not be called weak, I don't think the exact amount of &quot;HP per Second&quot; they can dish it's going to break the suspension of disbelief.<br /> <br /> But that's just mi opinion, obviously other people would care a lot more than me about the DPS of their Archmasters and place less weight on other feats of magick.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A little of-subject, but am i the only one who interprets rotes as NEVER counting as harried spells? </div> </div> </div>It was my understanding that they shouldn't count as hurried spells. That's supposed to be their one 100% RAW advantage, isn't it?. I don't remember how DA dealt with that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Oh, i'm sure i've asked this before, but how do i separate the text iam quoting into many parts? Like you did in the text i am quoting right now </div> </div> </div>Copy and paste the text of the answer you want to quote. Then higlight the pasted text and click the button with quotation marks that's inside the advanced editor bar (the <u>A</u> button shows the bar), it should surround the text with [QUOTE] markers.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 03-05-2020, 07:34 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-05T06:17:47-08:00","id":1372952,"title":"","content":"For me, the fact that Foundation only goes up to 5 goes hand in hand with the pool being Foundation + Pillar, whereas Arete going up to 10 goes with the pool being just Arete: the former starts you with larger pools up front, but caps them at the same ten dots that the Arete pool is capped at. The idea of allowing Foundation to exceed 5 (letting the pool offer up to 15 dice) is troubling; letting Pillars exceed 5 as well is just plain crazy. <br /> <br /> Conversely, I generally don't consider Archspheres to be a thing. What you can do at Mastery is already insane; <i>if</i> there's a \u201csixth dot\u201d in a Sphere, it's not something you can reach just by spending experience points: it should be akin to undergoing a second Awakening, putting you in the same general territory as achieving Ascension. And its effects should be comparable to Rank 6+ spirits, which are no longer in the realm of what player characters can directly challenge: it should basically be an \u201cI win\u201d button against anything short of the likes of the Wyrm or God. In short, it's not really a sixth dot so much as it removes the character from the realm of playability due to sheer overpowering. <br /> <br /> And I tend to treat \u201carch-Pillars\u201d the same way: there are no \u201csixth dots\u201d for either Foundation or Pillar, not without achieving something akin to Ascension; and at that point, you're not playing Mage anymore.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-05T07:01:15-08:00","id":1372956,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1364694-modern-foundations-and-pillars?p=1372952#post1372952\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For me, the fact that Foundation only goes up to 5 goes hand in hand with the pool being Foundation + Pillar, whereas Arete going up to 10 goes with the pool being just Arete: the former starts you with larger pools up front, but caps them at the same ten dots that the Arete pool is capped at. The idea of allowing Foundation to exceed 5 (letting the pool offer up to 15 dice) is troubling; letting Pillars exceed 5 as well is just plain crazy.<br /> <br /> Conversely, I generally don't consider Archspheres to be a thing. What you can do at Mastery is already insane; <i>if</i> there's a \u201csixth dot\u201d in a Sphere, it's not something you can reach just by spending experience points: it should be akin to undergoing a second Awakening, putting you in the same general territory as achieving Ascension. And its effects should be comparable to Rank 6+ spirits, which are no longer in the realm of what player characters can directly challenge: it should basically be an \u201cI win\u201d button against anything short of the likes of the Wyrm or God. In short, it's not really a sixth dot so much as it removes the character from the realm of playability due to sheer overpowering.<br /> <br /> And I tend to treat \u201carch-Pillars\u201d the same way: there are no \u201csixth dots\u201d for either Foundation or Pillar, not without achieving something akin to Ascension; and at that point, you're not playing Mage anymore.</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem with that view is that Ascension and Archmastery are most explicitly NOT the same thing in this game. (albeit many in-game people believe it to be so)<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> That being said, i dislike Masters of the Art's method of handling it. I much prefer the Horizon system. Same with pillars","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-05T07:03:54-08:00","id":1372957,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>KnightFenrirWulfhart</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1364694-modern-foundations-and-pillars?p=1369455#post1369455\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was thinking about it lately and I realized that Arete is literally just a newer Foundation and the Spheres are just vaguer Pillars. The whole idea of there being only 4 Pillars isn't real in world, its just there for mechanical balance. I don't believe there's really any true metaphysical difference between them. Mechanically it comes from the fact that Spheres and Arete were developed first.<br /> I mean Foundations and Pillars are actually must better to conceptualize Ascension with, and it fits lore much better to me.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes indeed someone even made an Arete Foundation with appropriate pillars<br /> <br /> Pillars are more vague than spheres however, less scientific . Similar to how using chimerstry and fortitude you could make a combo discipline that, say, creates &quot;hard&quot; illusions. Pillars can work like that too,","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-05T08:14:18-08:00","id":1372973,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1364694-modern-foundations-and-pillars?p=1372956#post1372956\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The problem with that view is that Ascension and Archmastery are most explicitly NOT the same thing in this game. (albeit many in-game people believe it to be so)</div> </div> </div> </div>That's why I compare it to Ascension, without equating them. This is one part of Masters of the Art that I like: its taxonomy of the different ways to move beyond being a mage. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1364694-modern-foundations-and-pillars?p=1372956#post1372956\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That being said, i dislike Masters of the Art's method of handling it. I much prefer the Horizon system. Same with pillars</div> </div> </div> </div>For the most part, I agree. <br /> And in fact what I described above largely builds on the Horizon approach; I just shift it from \u201cthe sixth dot costs 120 experience points\u201d to \u201cthe secret to attaining the \u2018sixth dot\u2019 involves something more than experience points\u201d, and I put characters who have achieved this level of power in the same category as Greater Spiritual Entities, or at least on par with the likes of the Incarnae: that is, they're not something you stat up on a regular character sheet. Basically, I take the advice given in Horizon about the Storyteller being well within his rights to just say \u201cno\u201d to Archspheres; but I do it by linking the \u201csixth dot\u201d to modes of being that can't be achieved without the mage transforming into something that's no longer a mage.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"l